e\n     2\t\n     3\tThis document is the source of truth for how the Yalla Cont
rol Plane Postgres\n     4\tdatabase is backed up, restored, and rehearsed. It i
s owned by Backend\n     5\tOperations. Anything that contradicts this file in a
wiki, ticket, or Slack\n     6\tthread is wrong.\n     7\t\n     8\tStory: **BE-
0039** — *Implement backup and restore plan for control-plane DB*.\n     9\t\n  
 10\t## Why this matters\n    11\t\n    12\tThe control-plane database is the so
urce of truth for every tenant, user, API\n    13\tkey, scoped grant, project, e
nvironment, service, deployment, quota, audit\n    14\tevent, and provisioning-j
ob row. A lost database means lost desired state,\n    15\tunauditable history, 
and an outage that no Dokploy redeploy can repair.\n    16\tDokploy itself can b
e re-provisioned from desired state; the desired state\n    17\tcannot be recons
tructed from Dokploy.\n    18\t\n    19\tTreat this runbook as production-critic
al and update it alongside any change\n    20\tthat affects backup frequency, re
tention, encryption, or restore steps.\n    21\t\n    22\t## Backup contract\n  
 23\t\n    24\t| Field                | Value                                   
                                   |\n    25\t| -------------------- | ---------
------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n    26\t| 
Frequency            | Full logical backup every **24 hours** at 02:00 UTC      
                   |\n    27\t| Incremental WAL      | Continuous WAL archival e
very **60 seconds** (RPO ≤ 1 minute)                |\n    28\t| Retention — ful
l     | **30 daily snapshots**, **12 weekly snapshots**, **6 monthly snapshots**
    |\n    29\t| Retention — WAL      | **48 hours** of contiguous WAL (covers a
ny restore point within 2 days)     |\n    30\t| Storage              | Encrypte
d object storage, **AES-256-GCM at rest**, KMS-managed key          |\n    31\t|
Transport            | TLS 1.2+ end-to-end; the backup agent never writes plaint
ext to local disk  |\n    32\t| Access               | Backup bucket is private;
restore requires break-glass MFA + ticketed reason |\n    33\t| RTO target      
    | **≤ 30 minutes** to bring a restored standby up                           
  |\n    34\t| RPO target           | **≤ 1 minute** (via WAL stream)           
                                  |\n    35\t| Owner on call        | Backend Op
erations rotation (PagerDuty: `yalla-be-ops`)                     |\n    36\t\n 
  37\tThe frequency, retention, RTO, and RPO are the customer-facing contract.\n
   38\tChanging any of them is a breaking change to the SLO and requires a\n    
39\tfollow-up PRD story.\n    40\t\n    41\t## Encryption expectations\n    42\t
\n    43\t1. **At rest**: every object the backup pipeline writes is encrypted u
nder a\n    44\t   KMS-managed key. The key is rotated every 90 days; rotation i
s the\n    45\t   operator\'s responsibility, not Yalla\'s.\n    46\t2. **In tra
nsit**: the backup agent connects to Postgres over TLS using the\n    47\t   sam
e certificate bundle the control-plane API uses. The pipeline ships\n    48\t   
the encrypted backup to object storage over HTTPS. Plaintext WAL must\n    49\t 
 never be written to disk; the agent streams from Postgres directly into\n    50
\t   the encrypted stream.\n    51\t3. **Key access**: only the backup agent\'s 
IAM principal and a separate\n    52\t   "restore-operator" principal can read t
he backups. The control-plane API\n    53\t   and worker processes have **no** r
ead access to the backup bucket.\n    54\t4. **Auditability**: every restore (su
ccess or failure) emits an audit\n    55\t   event with code `operations.backup.
restore` and a redacted hint naming\n    56\t   the restore point. Failed restor
es are paged.\n    57\t\n    58\t## Secrets / redaction\n    59\t\n    60\tThe b
ackup pipeline must **never** write secrets to logs, the status file, or\n    61
\tthe destination bucket\'s metadata. Specifically:\n    62\t\n    63\t- The Pos
